It adds a virtual link.

101
00:06:53,840 --> 00:06:56,520
It adds a device.

102
00:06:57,190 --> 00:07:02,960
Here, it's specifying the
device types or link types.

103
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:06,650
We could have a bridge, a bond.

104
00:07:06,650 --> 00:07:10,370
Some other ones we
talked about, VETH,

105
00:07:10,370 --> 00:07:14,855
VLAN, VXLAN, GRE.

106
00:07:14,855 --> 00:07:18,180
There's a bunch of
different options.

107
00:07:20,350 --> 00:07:23,480
Recall the different
types of devices?

108
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:28,505
We saw that all of those exist
as one of those options.

109
00:07:28,505 --> 00:07:31,610
And then each uses IP link a bit

110
00:07:31,610 --> 00:07:32,930
differently because they all

111
00:07:32,930 --> 00:07:35,850
have different possible
configurations.

112
00:07:35,850 --> 00:07:39,670
If we come back to this.

113
00:07:39,670 --> 00:07:43,225
Now here, say under VLAN,

114
00:07:43,225 --> 00:07:48,260
this is what setting

115
00:07:48,260 --> 00:07:52,325
a device or adding a device
of type VLAN would include.

116
00:07:52,325 --> 00:07:58,460
We're going to include
the device, the name,

117
00:07:58,460 --> 00:08:01,415
and we're going to specify
that it's type VLAN,

118
00:08:01,415 --> 00:08:08,345
then there's VLAN specific
things like the VLAN ID.

119
00:08:08,345 --> 00:08:11,360
So these are all
different things and if

120
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:13,925
you keep searching,

121
00:08:13,925 --> 00:08:17,210
there's probably different
ones that exist.

122
00:08:17,210 --> 00:08:20,030
So here's for VXLAN
and this would

123
00:08:20,030 --> 00:08:25,050
have VXLAN specific parameters.

124
00:08:27,490 --> 00:08:30,590
Now, how could you use this?

125
00:08:30,590 --> 00:08:33,575
Let's see full examples of this.

126
00:08:33,575 --> 00:08:35,150
We mentioned bridge.

127
00:08:35,150 --> 00:08:36,950
What exactly is a bridge?

128
00:08:36,950 --> 00:08:39,785
A bridge is the device that

129
00:08:39,785 --> 00:08:43,355
effectively implements
a learning switch.

130
00:08:43,355 --> 00:08:48,860
We've seen in the past some
pictures that look like this.

131
00:08:48,860 --> 00:08:50,780
We have a host and this is

132
00:08:50,780 --> 00:08:54,065
representing a physical switch.

133
00:08:54,065 --> 00:08:56,000
We know we could plug this in

134
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:57,650
if this is a learning switch,

135
00:08:57,650 --> 00:09:02,880
when this tries to
send out traffic,

136
00:09:03,040 --> 00:09:05,270
the learning switch doesn't have

137
00:09:05,270 --> 00:09:08,120
any MAC in its MAC table.

138
00:09:08,120 --> 00:09:11,690
Any entries for the
particular destination

139
00:09:11,690 --> 00:09:12,980
, it'll broadcast that.

140
00:09:12,980 --> 00:09:16,475
It'll flood it on all outputs.

141
00:09:16,475 --> 00:09:21,420
Then whenever there's a
response, it'll learn that.

142
00:09:21,940 --> 00:09:24,895
A all of this is
working at layer 2,

143
00:09:24,895 --> 00:09:28,065
so it will all be MAC addresses.

144
00:09:28,065 --> 00:09:30,815
How do you use it in Linux?

145
00:09:30,815 --> 00:09:32,930
Well, we're going
to create a device.

146
00:09:32,930 --> 00:09:35,375
We're going to call
it whatever we want.

147
00:09:35,375 --> 00:09:37,835
On the pictures,
bridge 0 or br0.

148
00:09:37,835 --> 00:09:43,165
Then we're connect
Ethernet devices to it,

149
00:09:43,165 --> 00:09:47,650
effectively inside of
Linux, creating a bridge.

150
00:09:47,650 --> 00:09:50,860
You first create
the bridge device,

151
00:09:50,860 --> 00:09:58,010
then you make the other
devices slaves to that bridge.

152
00:09:58,010 --> 00:10:03,725
Shown here, we're going
to say IP link add name,

153
00:10:03,725 --> 00:10:07,470
mybridge; type, bridge.

154
00:10:07,510 --> 00:10:11,285
It starts in the down
state, it's not active.

155
00:10:11,285 --> 00:10:13,130
We first have to bring it up.

156
00:10:13,130 --> 00:10:16,010
We say IP link set the
name of the bridge,

157
00:10:16,010 --> 00:10:19,250
which is my bridge or
the name of the device.

158
00:10:19,250 --> 00:10:24,150
Then the state we want to
bring it up into, which is up.

159
00:10:25,090 --> 00:10:28,280
Then we're going to use

160
00:10:28,280 --> 00:10:31,595
the set command for
these other devices,

161
00:10:31,595 --> 00:10:33,755
eth1, eth2, eth3,

162
00:10:33,755 --> 00:10:38,160
to make its master, my bridge.

163
00:10:40,750 --> 00:10:43,460
So this picture
doesn't match it.

164
00:10:43,460 --> 00:10:45,005
If this said my bridge,

165
00:10:45,005 --> 00:10:46,610
that's exactly what this set

166
00:10:46,610 --> 00:10:49,080
of commands would be building.

167
00:10:49,630 --> 00:10:52,535
Let's look at VLAN,

168
00:10:52,535 --> 00:10:54,515
and we saw the
main page briefly.

169
00:10:54,515 --> 00:10:59,030
You can go look on your own
to see it at your own pace.

170
00:10:59,030 --> 00:11:03,215
But what is a VLAN device?

171
00:11:03,215 --> 00:11:06,545
It's a device that
adds VLAN tagging.

172
00:11:06,545 --> 00:11:09,890
VLAN is useful for

173
00:11:09,890 --> 00:11:14,015
enabling isolation in a
shared Layer 2 network.

174
00:11:14,015 --> 00:11:16,085
Let's say we had

175
00:11:16,085 --> 00:11:18,515
a shared Layer 2 network

176
00:11:18,515 --> 00:11:20,685
represented by this
single switch.

177
00:11:20,685 --> 00:11:22,840
We had four hosts on it,

178
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:25,060
but we wanted to
isolate their traffic.

179
00:11:25,060 --> 00:11:26,830
We wanted only host 1 and

180
00:11:26,830 --> 00:11:29,005
host 2 to be able to
talk to each other,

181
00:11:29,005 --> 00:11:30,340
and then host 3 and host

182
00:11:30,340 --> 00:11:31,930
4 to be able to
talk to each other,

183
00:11:31,930 --> 00:11:34,890
but not between them.

184
00:11:34,890 --> 00:11:37,355
We could use VLAN.

185
00:11:37,355 --> 00:11:40,175
We can assign host 1 and 2,

186
00:11:40,175 --> 00:11:44,840
all their traffic could
be a particular VLAN ID,

187
00:11:44,840 --> 00:11:48,690
then host 3 and 4, could
get a different one.

188
00:11:49,180 --> 00:11:53,465
How this shows up in
the Ethernet header,

189
00:11:53,465 --> 00:12:02,645
there's a little extra that
it can specify the VLAN ID's.

190
00:12:02,645 --> 00:12:07,370
How you would create a
device like this is,

191
00:12:07,370 --> 00:12:09,950
you would basically
do it on top of

192
00:12:09,950 --> 00:12:13,985
a physical network device.

193
00:12:13,985 --> 00:12:16,625
We would say IP link ad,

194
00:12:16,625 --> 00:12:18,200
we're going to specify

195
00:12:18,200 --> 00:12:22,415
the Ethernet device to
associate the VLAN with.

196
00:12:22,415 --> 00:12:24,815
In this case it's zero.

197
00:12:24,815 --> 00:12:26,570
We're going to give it a name,

198
00:12:26,570 --> 00:12:28,895
the VLAN device a name,

199
00:12:28,895 --> 00:12:34,315
the convention is to
whatever the device name is,

200
00:12:34,315 --> 00:12:37,465
eth0 then dot something.

201
00:12:37,465 --> 00:12:41,755
Where dot something is
going to be the VLAN ID.

202
00:12:41,755 --> 00:12:46,485
We're going to say that
this type of type vlan,

203
00:12:46,485 --> 00:12:49,970
then the VLAN
specific parameters

204
00:12:49,970 --> 00:12:52,520
which we're saying ID 2.

205
00:12:52,520 --> 00:12:56,825
Then any traffic that
goes through eth0.2

206
00:12:56,825 --> 00:13:01,655
device will get
tagged with an ID 2.

207
00:13:01,655 --> 00:13:05,850
It'll set this part

208
00:13:06,670 --> 00:13:12,630
here specifically with
that value of two.

209
00:13:13,030 --> 00:13:17,405
A tunnel is something
that encapsulates

210
00:13:17,405 --> 00:13:22,290
traffic for transmission
over some network.

211
00:13:22,960 --> 00:13:32,990
Over here, we have one device
and here we have one node,

212
00:13:32,990 --> 00:13:36,035
let's call it a
host and over here,

213
00:13:36,035 --> 00:13:37,835
we have another host.

214
00:13:37,835 --> 00:13:41,630
Within that host,
let's say each of

215
00:13:41,630 --> 00:13:46,080
them has a network card
that has eth1 on it.

216
00:13:47,710 --> 00:13:52,070
These might be
across the internet.

217
00:13:52,070 --> 00:13:55,040
What we're going to do
is create a new device

218
00:13:55,040 --> 00:13:58,415
called gen0 for geneve,

219
00:13:58,415 --> 00:14:02,120
where any traffic
that goes into it,

220
00:14:02,120 --> 00:14:04,640
we'll get some extra headers put

221
00:14:04,640 --> 00:14:06,800
onto it such that it's creating

222
00:14:06,800 --> 00:14:09,410
this tunnel to the other side

223
00:14:09,410 --> 00:14:12,185
so that it could go
out over this network,

224
00:14:12,185 --> 00:14:14,105
reach the other side.

225
00:14:14,105 --> 00:14:16,145
When it reaches this device,

226
00:14:16,145 --> 00:14:20,130
it can pull off all that
header information.

227
00:14:20,290 --> 00:14:23,420
This is work in geneve tunnels,

228
00:14:23,420 --> 00:14:26,000
work across an IP network.

229
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,820
It adds an extra set of headers.

230
00:14:30,250 --> 00:14:33,875
You can see it adds some
extra headers which

231
00:14:33,875 --> 00:14:39,230
allows you to transit
it across the network.

232
00:14:39,230 --> 00:14:41,555
That's not the
local area network.

233
00:14:41,555 --> 00:14:44,765
If you had it directly
on this eth1,

234
00:14:44,765 --> 00:14:47,550
that's all it would
be able to do.

235
00:14:47,740 --> 00:14:50,720
Had the traffic go
through just eth1,

236
00:14:50,720 --> 00:14:53,105
it would only be able to talk to

237
00:14:53,105 --> 00:14:55,850
that network unless they

238
00:14:55,850 --> 00:14:58,835
had more destination
and so forth.

239
00:14:58,835 --> 00:15:02,585
What we're doing is we're
using an IP link add again,

240
00:15:02,585 --> 00:15:04,820
we're giving it a name,

241
00:15:04,820 --> 00:15:09,455
gen0, we're give it type geneve.

242
00:15:09,455 --> 00:15:11,720
Then the geneve specific,

243
00:15:11,720 --> 00:15:14,915
we're going to give
it an ID of 55.

244
00:15:14,915 --> 00:15:17,285
We're going to say
the remote end of

245
00:15:17,285 --> 00:15:20,555
this tunnel that's over here,

246
00:15:20,555 --> 00:15:25,410
it's going to be 1.2.3,4
so my IP address.

247
00:15:26,350 --> 00:15:29,390
If you're interested in that,

248
00:15:29,390 --> 00:15:31,790
just look more into
geneve tunnel.

249
00:15:31,790 --> 00:15:33,500
What we're trying to
convey here is more of

250
00:15:33,500 --> 00:15:37,710
the syntax of the command
and what you can do with it.

251
00:15:38,140 --> 00:15:41,270
Now that's the Add.

252
00:15:41,270 --> 00:15:43,595
Then finally Delete.

253
00:15:43,595 --> 00:15:46,625
If you don't want it,
the device anymore,

254
00:15:46,625 --> 00:15:47,690
you could just say IP link

255
00:15:47,690 --> 00:15:51,300
delete and then give
it the device name.

256
00:15:52,720 --> 00:15:56,675
Now let's go into a demo,

257
00:15:56,675 --> 00:15:59,240
just to show what's going on,

258
00:15:59,240 --> 00:16:06,380
let's look at creating
container lab deployment.

259
00:16:06,380 --> 00:16:09,515
Again, let's go over,

